73
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:34,980
This is a big university.

74
00:02:34,980 --> 00:02:37,060
So, there might only be a few hundred professors of

75
00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:39,100
Chinese literature in the entire country.

76
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:41,620
Yet, how many psychologists have we got in LA?

77
00:02:41,620 --> 00:02:44,380
How many psychology professors are there?

78
00:02:44,380 --> 00:02:47,180
So, thousands and thousands.

79
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:49,300
Right. So, given this,

80
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:53,340
even if only one percent of psychology professors were Chinese,

81
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:56,620
and 100 percent of Chinese literature professors were Chinese,

82
00:02:56,620 --> 00:02:59,500
it could still easily be the case that the probability that

83
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:03,820
a Chinese professor is a professor of psychology,

84
00:03:03,820 --> 00:03:06,340
be way greater. And this is the problem.

85
00:03:06,340 --> 00:03:08,500
Again, ignoring the fact that some things are just much more

86
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:11,580
frequent than other things when you assess probabilities.

87
00:03:11,580 --> 00:03:15,980
So, representative heuristic is just using

88
00:03:15,980 --> 00:03:18,700
the match with stereotypes to estimate probabilities,

89
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:20,340
which can be totally reasonable.

90
00:03:20,340 --> 00:03:24,980
But the bias is when you do it without adjusting for these base rates.

91
00:03:24,980 --> 00:03:27,860
And just remembering, some things are just more common to begin with.

92
00:03:27,860 --> 00:03:31,540
So, the fact that something is relatively likely,

93
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:35,260
given something doesn't mean that it is absolutely likely.

94
00:03:35,260 --> 00:03:40,460
All right. So, there's a classic experiment along these lines,

